[{:db/id "q-78983/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e88edebc-10aa-577c-9885-d56fe5e6160f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9a518d61-c933-5b78-b9d9-8e84118fb384" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "e0db6157-98a9-567b-b3ad-b81cceac2e4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "be612e63-2e38-5877-b165-01182404ce82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983/selection/6ee96c99c6020a8a0675f054530cea7b5db72506dc41f7e364f7485717e0d433" :answer/id #uuid "9ae10881-e54e-5906-9ce5-d2eac7acf29a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local minima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f55c3cf-eb7e-55ec-9964-c22648d9cd49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78983/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-78983/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-78983/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-78983/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-78983/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-78983/selection/6ee96c99c6020a8a0675f054530cea7b5db72506dc41f7e364f7485717e0d433"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78983" :question/id #uuid "5b083dc1-b3f3-517b-a6eb-d7231518e3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-78983" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-3x+5$ have a local minimum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({x}^{2}-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =3((-2)^{2}-1)=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3((0)^{2}-1)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3((2)^{2}-1)=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-78983/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344272/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "deb22cef-be44-5461-9403-99d0fb746b14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344272/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0910c24f-a334-5163-bcc5-85d7ff3202bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344272/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344272/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344272" :question/id #uuid "02733b2a-13b7-528a-9373-3c369c69bd24" :question/math-academy-id "q-344272" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-9x-11$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3{x}^{2}+6x-9.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+6x-9=0\\,⟹\\,x=-3,\\,1.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-3$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-4) & =3(-4)^{2}+6(-4)-9=15>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =3(0)^{2}+6(0)-9=-9<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =3(2)^{2}+6(2)-9=15>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local minimum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344272/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344272"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "b7b76953-7cdd-5176-9bad-4c05b0ca7cb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "827e9847-0070-52a6-95e1-7794ccecf414" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "66a03397-9f2f-56bd-8d80-5e198dc9d668" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "1dbd6480-4929-5949-a0b1-a153881b8a28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "99b1c3a7-5dfa-54d7-b84a-2651e9b4aea1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39d8d08d-8ae7-56e2-9446-ea879b820394" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-21467/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-21467/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-21467/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-21467/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-21467/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-21467/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-21467" :question/id #uuid "f13d0a0f-3535-547f-bf2f-0daa5e0b2ddb" :question/math-academy-id "q-21467" :question/problem "For the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-12x+6,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-2$ is a local maximum\n- $x=1$ is a local minimum\n- $x=1$ is a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}+6x-12 \\\\  & =6({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =6(x+2)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(x+2)(x-1)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-2$ and $x=1.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-3,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-3) & =6(-3+2)(-3-1)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =6(0+2)(0-1)=-12<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =6(2+2)(2-1)=24>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $-2$ | $(-2,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-3$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-2$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local minimum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-21467/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-21467"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection/205551d2aac039ff73a6b5420ccbf84acf378960d40f2fbc8076d8ab6f96bc8d" :answer/id #uuid "c32a91b6-0aa1-5a7a-ada6-cbe1f5a62563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection/c5a0007895a0b33276781a155ddb1d83be3c93a909759a21526781541fd41e06" :answer/id #uuid "c78a7de0-8f84-52dc-a2b2-893436a724aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-1/2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection/14aaad26aef1140510e3edebc455e7bbac730c62423ecca9f66d640a392befd9" :answer/id #uuid "147d98de-aec3-5443-83a9-b7444200833f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection/b9458cd3c13c607d124bf055e0cc1d00255dd23f423ecfcb838a04c4a6a16110" :answer/id #uuid "c9e91357-5531-50a8-802d-aceb74c63121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{-2/3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "bd9a3518-974e-5c8e-af40-3c5bbcab09d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ef257078-8c2b-5897-9e3a-55eb8c36d31a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-22504/selection/205551d2aac039ff73a6b5420ccbf84acf378960d40f2fbc8076d8ab6f96bc8d" :answer-field/choices ["q-22504/selection/205551d2aac039ff73a6b5420ccbf84acf378960d40f2fbc8076d8ab6f96bc8d" "q-22504/selection/c5a0007895a0b33276781a155ddb1d83be3c93a909759a21526781541fd41e06" "q-22504/selection/14aaad26aef1140510e3edebc455e7bbac730c62423ecca9f66d640a392befd9" "q-22504/selection/b9458cd3c13c607d124bf055e0cc1d00255dd23f423ecfcb838a04c4a6a16110" "q-22504/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-22504" :question/id #uuid "4f2f830a-8fd4-5def-9efa-5e26534df70d" :question/math-academy-id "q-22504" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty )$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot {x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\cdot 2x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ on $(0,\\infty )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & ={e}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x={e}^{1/3},e$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}({e}^{1/3}) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{1/3}}{{({e}^{1/3})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{3e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(e) & =\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e}{{e}^{3}}=-\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,{e}^{1/2})$ | ${e}^{1/2}$ | $({e}^{1/2},\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | ${e}^{1/3}$ |  | $e$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x={e}^{1/2},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x={e}^{1/2}$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x={e}^{1/2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-22504/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-22504"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" :answer/id #uuid "5e4a1e9d-c345-59a1-9a67-1560862c2b3d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "bfce3109-00b3-5d91-8b9e-8d33a75183bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "868c0540-12d0-5309-83e6-3f5a41dec19d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "32dbf757-0832-5821-aec0-80773ba20a3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a4762953-66f1-57c4-a231-5fe9f0006370" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ff2da374-3b73-50db-bed3-652771d01274" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49033/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-49033/selection/74053cd8f212994373f56db80fbf0eade2779b20bfbeb0bc87c25a146cc918d6" "q-49033/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-49033/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-49033/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-49033/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49033" :question/id #uuid "ee2cc97e-2362-5237-bd9e-282271c99f6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-49033" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{x}$ have a local maximum?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(1-x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-{e}^{x}=-x{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{e}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{1}=-e<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-49033/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49033"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344405/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "5b28d505-963c-58d1-847b-5cf99aa4bff7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344405/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "0d7ead62-956d-5576-8252-9eee787e03a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344405/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1ae6b923-49f9-5959-92e9-746e4296e141" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344405/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344405/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-344405/field-1/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344405" :question/id #uuid "1aa25549-7d3d-5f53-8902-eddd426eb705" :question/math-academy-id "q-344405" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}\\,=\\,\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =\\frac{1-(-2)^{2}}{{((-2)^{2}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1-4}{(5)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1-{0}^{2}}{{({0}^{2}+1)}^{2}}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1-{2}^{2}}{{({2}^{2}+1)}^{2}}=\\frac{1-4}{(5)^{2}}=-\\frac{3}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=1$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344405/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344405"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344388/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "46044faa-fd0f-523d-8558-4a0b6be2b490" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344388/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c217a49-4a04-5594-9b51-ea4e207ee936" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344388/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344388/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344388" :question/id #uuid "14ac5621-765a-5a3d-9c74-f6ac403575d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-344388" :question/problem "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{x}$ have a local minimum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local minimum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}-4x+1)\\cdot {e}^{x}+(2x-4){e}^{x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2x-3)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}(x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=-1$ and $x=3$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ={e}^{-2}(-1)(-5)=5{e}^{-2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}(1)(-3)=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & ={e}^{4}(5)(1)=5{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local maximum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local minimum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local minimum at $x=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344388/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbad38a0-e6cb-563a-8e9c-1cee08397083"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344388"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection/9ea2aed4d134764520f8991d3d65dad8705c3f681ce996009e4c50616b916109" :answer/id #uuid "f21e2a5c-f0a1-54b1-9978-2ddcd71f622c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection/b13a6edf537fadd0a364530d0b2e200a647151a1c4f05f8b2781ec79f75f828d" :answer/id #uuid "4cd32917-3023-579d-b228-6de320190b02" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection/33e3cbeabb17c22b2afd6b562d99830449b6385abd5db040d80912961fec70e4" :answer/id #uuid "2aba295c-5d9b-50b7-bdf1-35bbc62f7ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x)$ has no local extrema on $x\\in [0,\\infty )$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection/b05cf04f512b7381b2bd6e2db7cb466a53863bc8f43c93e0853537ecb5d62815" :answer/id #uuid "943136fd-3665-53ba-b52e-6835f64b4c5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection/724d4d30acf4cb637aae20543f65a0144cc6acb230830735f696d9f119e968a4" :answer/id #uuid "082cfc77-14e4-56e6-b30d-af3caed2574a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum at $x=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4001918b-d326-5595-821e-1d2487652b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-49025/selection/33e3cbeabb17c22b2afd6b562d99830449b6385abd5db040d80912961fec70e4" :answer-field/choices ["q-49025/selection/9ea2aed4d134764520f8991d3d65dad8705c3f681ce996009e4c50616b916109" "q-49025/selection/b13a6edf537fadd0a364530d0b2e200a647151a1c4f05f8b2781ec79f75f828d" "q-49025/selection/33e3cbeabb17c22b2afd6b562d99830449b6385abd5db040d80912961fec70e4" "q-49025/selection/b05cf04f512b7381b2bd6e2db7cb466a53863bc8f43c93e0853537ecb5d62815" "q-49025/selection/724d4d30acf4cb637aae20543f65a0144cc6acb230830735f696d9f119e968a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-49025" :question/id #uuid "ac621d85-6618-59f0-8173-be7a4706164c" :question/math-academy-id "q-49025" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x-1)^{3}{e}^{x}+3(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}{e}^{x}(x-1+3) \\\\  & =(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}(x+2){e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${e}^{x}>0$ for all values of $x.$\n\nSo, $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =(0-1)^{2}(0+2){e}^{0}=2>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =(2-1)^{2}(2+2){e}^{2}=4{e}^{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $[0,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $[0,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-49025/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-49025"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection/d7740b3209e4982b66acb6d2910c875c237d314d77efc17a891afdb5e4e7bc68" :answer/id #uuid "39acc041-7f03-58cc-8d8b-a10f6b78c616" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection/2390dfcf5e634c9595afa54d563380c521b3100e7c6de85edacb4d27d5d35606" :answer/id #uuid "acc58338-ffd3-5655-9cbe-3d640666a7f8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection/bda81595d0d5d7cc570eb70de6e710b07ac04a7248b49b496bb5549988fa4eae" :answer/id #uuid "a93f83b3-d67e-563b-be91-54bbed67199f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection/9beb57cbc1f495bfd97113cb30d69659b5f1248aeb7339679fa374c7c7a663e4" :answer/id #uuid "5b33665a-1d58-5ff6-a744-5babee0c2b71" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "ddd8d1bb-a88c-5f25-a74e-013aa6c6fd68" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "82cece45-c39d-5eb8-a9be-12814d5d653d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113768/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113768/selection/d7740b3209e4982b66acb6d2910c875c237d314d77efc17a891afdb5e4e7bc68" "q-113768/selection/2390dfcf5e634c9595afa54d563380c521b3100e7c6de85edacb4d27d5d35606" "q-113768/selection/bda81595d0d5d7cc570eb70de6e710b07ac04a7248b49b496bb5549988fa4eae" "q-113768/selection/9beb57cbc1f495bfd97113cb30d69659b5f1248aeb7339679fa374c7c7a663e4" "q-113768/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113768" :question/id #uuid "d734b4ff-5976-5354-96f9-b1ca59071265" :question/math-academy-id "q-113768" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x+3}{{x}^{3}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(2x-3){x}^{3}-3({x}^{2}-3x+3){x}^{2}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}-3x-3{x}^{2}+9x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}^{2}+6x-9}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{x}^{4}}\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nSo, $x=3$ is the only stationary point of $f(x)$ on $(0,\\infty ).$ Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=1,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{(1-3)^{2}}{{1}^{4}}=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{(4-3)^{2}}{{4}^{4}}=-\\frac{1}{256}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=3.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=3$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nTherefore, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema on $(0,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-113768/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113768"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" :answer/id #uuid "a733457a-c441-5230-925d-a12a204900b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection/8868f02215a5337e2a5129e75503fbec783bb64f355b374ffdd731bd6e356bdb" :answer/id #uuid "a29e3487-e0d5-5619-942e-66e55b9015a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=-1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" :answer/id #uuid "6741f1d8-0e76-5d19-ab0b-7847d8c593c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local maximum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer/id #uuid "06c4c9eb-f86e-5536-b095-bb6cd4e29bdb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310" :answer/id #uuid "ee3d28cc-1ab6-5f34-a650-78711ffa2294" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "There is a local minimum at $x=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a3c5888-f08b-57a9-8445-91e772da6c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-113762/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" :answer-field/choices ["q-113762/selection/d9b70cb012b8a98348e0eb2b076c0ffaa03d2f9b5ed0b778c333cec60818df33" "q-113762/selection/8868f02215a5337e2a5129e75503fbec783bb64f355b374ffdd731bd6e356bdb" "q-113762/selection/f6a9948068396877505e54f66bfd517f08252fd5fcfe067bfbdff6bc25b00dda" "q-113762/selection/c112c665879534c7ad1d1042a8b28b7e5f3f70117c93b42716ffdd8ce86d808a" "q-113762/selection/acc83c74f03748b2e35d16f5b1d34df696d35c1cc9214487ece6a3d7d0147310"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-113762" :question/id #uuid "d9a92603-1c58-5381-9a97-44ce967ac9d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-113762" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-3x+5,$ which of the following statements are true?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}+6x-3 \\\\  & =-3({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =-3(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3(x-1)^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nSo $x=1$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=0,2$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0-1)^{2}=-3<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =-3(2-1)^{2}=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $0$ |  | $2$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ |  | $↘$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=1.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=1$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-113762/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ff7c597d-d4a4-5567-add6-eac77475b0d6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-113762"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
